WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.303 --> 00:00:02.640
(bright music)

2
00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.360
- [Narrator] It started over 50 years ago with an idea.

3
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.530
A satellite, orbiting Earth, observing our planet surface,

4
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:14.310
gathering data, day in, day out.

5
00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.310
That idea gave birth to the Landsat program,

6
00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:21.570
a partnership between NASA and the US Geological Survey,

7
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.130
the longest continuous record

8
00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.190
of Earth's land surface from space.

9
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:29.610
Landsat 1's launch in 1972 was the first link

10
00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.440
in a chain of eight satellites,

11
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.020
each one building upon the last.

12
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.841
And today, Landsat 9 carries that legacy forward.

13
00:00:37.841 --> 00:00:39.036
(bright music)

14
00:00:39.036 --> 00:00:43.320
- [Announcer] Two, one, ignition, and lift off!

15
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.450
- [Narrator] Since its launch in 2021,

16
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.820
Landsat 9 collects as many scenes per day

17
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.000
as Landsats Five and Seven, combined.

18
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.070
Working in tandem with Landsat 8,

19
00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.730
the pair now collect nearly 1,500 scenes daily,

20
00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.370
creating a complete map of the planet's land surface

21
00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.350
every eight days.

22
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.420
It's not just about scale,

23
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:04.890
it's about Landsat's ability

24
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.730
to revisit the same scene multiple times a month.

25
00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.110
With the pace of acquisitions,

26
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.020
Landsat 9 helps track seasonal shifts in crops,

27
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.080
the spread of wildfires, the aftermath of storms,

28
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.230
and even rapid changes in glaciers and coastlines.

29
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.080
More images means more data, fueling research,

30
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.483
and scientific applications around the world.

31
00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.980
While Landsat 9's main design is nearly identical

32
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.340
to Landsat 8's, it's able to collect data in greater detail,

33
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.010
thanks to an upgraded radiometric resolution.

34
00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.100
14-bit, instead of Landsat 8's 12-bit.

35
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.070
Think of it like upgrading from a box of 4,000 crayons

36
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.320
to one with 16,000.

37
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.190
Every shade captured, every subtle detail sharper.

38
00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.190
Landsat 9's quadrupled radiometric sensitivity

39
00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.420
makes a real difference

40
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.390
when capturing data over the planet's brightest surfaces,

41
00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.070
like snow and ice,

42
00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.820
revealing subtle changes that might otherwise go unnoticed.

43
00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.410
Shifts in ice extent,

44
00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.990
changes in how surfaces reflect sunlight,

45
00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.450
and even the growth of glacial lakes forming

46
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:07.833
where ice once stood.

47
00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.360
Seeing more shades of detail is powerful,

48
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.630
but it only matters if the picture itself is clear.

49
00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.360
Landsat 9 not only sharpens what we can detect,

50
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.220
it also cuts through the static,

51
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.680
delivering a stronger signal-to-noise ratio,

52
00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.590
which means images that have less interference.

53
00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.320
It's like trying to hear a whisper in a noisy room.

54
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.120
Landsat 9 quiets the static

55
00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.580
so we don't miss anything important.

56
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.500
And that clarity makes a difference,

57
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.020
especially over dark surfaces like water,

58
00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.790
which can harbor harmful algal blooms

59
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.050
that can spread quickly,

60
00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.570
threatening drinking water supplies, local wildlife,

61
00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.250
and even human safety.

62
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.830
By spotting these blooms with greater sensitivity,

63
00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.290
Landsat 9 gives communities and scientists

64
00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:52.263
more reliable and actionable information to respond.

65
00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:56.280
Landsat 9 doesn't clock out when the sun goes down.

66
00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.770
Its onboard thermal sensor, TIRS,

67
00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.010
measures our planet's surface heat even in the darkness.

68
00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.200
That means we can monitor urban heat islands,

69
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.650
volcanic hotspots, and water temperature at night.

70
00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.180
Since 2022,

71
00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.750
the US Geological Survey's special request data program

72
00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.510
has implemented the Landsat Extended Acquisition

73
00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:18.330
of the Poles, or LEAP for short,

74
00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:21.390
taking advantage of Landsat 9's ability to see in the dark

75
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.570
to acquire imagery in polar regions year-round,

76
00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:28.500
when the sun can set for up to six months at the poles.

77
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.847
Together with Landsat 8,

78
00:03:29.847 --> 00:03:32.580
the satellites can detect features like meltwater,

79
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.950
cracks, and even open water within ice

80
00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.840
under low light conditions.

81
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.700
The enhanced coverage helps scientists

82
00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.220
better monitor ice dynamics and seasonal changes

83
00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.940
in polar regions, detecting calving events, surface melt,

84
00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.303
and changes in sea ice extent even during the dark months.

85
00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.420
Landsat 9 isn't working alone.

86
00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.280
It's part of a global team of satellites

87
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.950
where collaboration across agencies and nations

88
00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.150
is giving us the clearest,

89
00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.880
most consistent view of Earth yet.

90
00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.180
NASA's Harmonized Landsat Sentinel-2 project fuses data

91
00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.860
from Landsat Eight and Nine,

92
00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.720
with that of the European Space Agency's

93
00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.300
Sentinel-2 A, B, and C satellites

94
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.840
to form a seamless, consistent surface reflectance record.

95
00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.730
In this virtual constellation,

96
00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.040
Landsat 9 contributes its spectral precision

97
00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.450
and calibrated data,

98
00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.390
helping enable global observations every one to two days

99
00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.190
at 30-meter resolution.

100
00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:26.040
Landsat 9's high-fidelity, radiometric stability,

101
00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.440
and continuity anchor HLS,

102
00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.450
ensuring that the fused product maintains

103
00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:33.483
the scientific integrity that Landsat users expect.

104
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:37.200
Landsat 9 is more than just today's mission.

105
00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.900
It's part of the foundation for the future.

106
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.270
Through the Sustainable Land Imaging Program,

107
00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:45.300
NASA and the US Geological Survey aims to preserve

108
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.100
our ability to keep a continuous,

109
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.280
reliable record of Earth's land for decades to come.

110
00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.140
That means not just flying satellites,

111
00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.270
but building the technology, partnerships,

112
00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:57.540
and planning needed to keep that record unbroken.

113
00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:58.620
Within SLI,

114
00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:02.130
NASA's Sustainable Land Imaging Technology initiative

115
00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.630
is testing new instruments

116
00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.640
that can make future emissions smaller,

117
00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.950
more capable, and more efficient.

118
00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.650
Landsat 9 is NASA's first SLI mission,

119
00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.150
and plays a key role here,

120
00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.640
setting the benchmark for data quality and coverage,

121
00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:15.960
proving what works today,

122
00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.420
and guiding the technologies of tomorrow.

123
00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.670
Its stability and precision are hallmarks

124
00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.290
of previous lessons learned,

125
00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.110
allowing scientists to trust the record across decades,

126
00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.330
and its success helps guide the innovations

127
00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:28.473
that will come next.

128
00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.690
For more than half a century,

129
00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.330
Landsat satellites have given us an unbroken record

130
00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.040
of our changing planet.

131
00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.270
In just four years,

132
00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.780
Landsat 9 has brought that vision into even sharper focus,

133
00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.990
capturing millions of scenes, advancing how we track water,

134
00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.390
ice, and land, and strengthening the world's

135
00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.730
longest Earth-observation record.

136
00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:51.570
It's not just another satellite in orbit, it's a bridge,

137
00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:54.840
carrying the Landsat legacy forward with enhanced technology

138
00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.910
while preparing the foundation for the future

139
00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.950
of sustainable land imaging.

140
00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:02.460
Because with Landsat, every image is more than a picture.

141
00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.710
It's a calibrated digital record,

142
00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.170
providing knowledge we can use to understand,

143
00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.803
protect, and sustain life on Earth.
